@meta
id: 04-01
title: The four keys under your fingers
chapter: 04
type: lesson
skills: hjkl
par: 15
minutes: 3
vscode: arrow keys, or a click on the line you want
open: customer.entity.ts

@brief
In normal mode the cursor moves on `h` `j` `k` `l`. They sit under the
four fingers of your right hand, exactly where they already rest. Left,
down, up, right — in that order, left to right on the keyboard.

`j` looks like it hangs down. `k` sticks up. That is the whole mnemonic,
and after a week you will not need it.

The arrow keys also work. They are not the lesson. Every time you reach
for them your hand leaves the home row and has to find its way back, and
you pay that cost hundreds of times a day.

This file is missing a semicolon at the end of the `name` field. Add it
and save.

@keys
| `h` | left |
| `j` | down |
| `k` | up |
| `l` | right |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
The cursor starts on line 1. The field you want is further down, so `j`
is the key you need — press it once per line.
--
Once you are on the right line, `A` puts you in insert mode at the end of
it, whatever column you were in. Then the semicolon, then `Esc`.
--
Three presses of `j`, then `A`, then `;`, then `Esc`, then `:wq`.

@solution
    j j j A ; <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Ten keystrokes. Notice what you did not do: you did not look for the end
of the line, because `A` found it for you, and you did not aim with a
mouse, because the line was three down and you said so.

Keep your fingers home. `j` is the index finger's neighbour, not a
journey. If you catch yourself counting presses of `j` past about five,
the next lesson has the fix — and it is not holding the key down.

@start customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string
  createdAt: Date;
};

@expected customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string;
  createdAt: Date;
};
